In both cases the <alkene> undergoes concerted <oxygen> transfer from <metachloroperbenzoic acid>. The <alkene> <pi bond> forms one C–O bond to the terminal <peroxide> <oxygen>; <electron> redistribution forms the second C–O bond, breaks O–O, and transfers the peracid <proton> to its <carbonyl> <oxygen> as the <carboxylic acid> <product (chemistry)> forms. This cyclic <epoxidation> pathway delivers both C–O bonds from one face, without a freely rotating <carbon> intermediate.

For 1-methylcyclohexene, the two <alkene> faces are <enantiotopic>. Attack from opposite faces gives the two mirror-related <epoxides>, and an <achiral> peracid in an <achiral> environment cannot distinguish their <Gibbs energies of activation>. **The <product (chemistry)> is racemic, 1:1.**

For the cis-fused bicyclic <alkene>, the junction configuration and its puckered ring <conformation> make approach from the two faces sterically different. The folded saturated ring shields the concave face; <oxygen> transfer preferentially approaches the more exposed convex face, directed away from that ring. The alternative attack places the incoming peracid closer to the folded skeleton. **The 95:5 ratio represents facial <diastereoselectivity>**, while concerted syn <oxygen> delivery is retained for either <product (chemistry)>. It should not be explained by free rotation or by one <epoxide> subsequently changing configuration. For scale only, if this ratio were measured under irreversible kinetic conditions at 298 K, it would correspond to $RT\ln(95/5)\simeq7.30\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ difference between the competing <Gibbs energies of activation>; the exam does not specify that <temperature>.

= c
{parent=B3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**E, with the axial anomeric <acetate>, hydrolyses faster.** The tert-butyl <substituent> strongly favours the chair in which it is equatorial, so the two <substrate (chemistry)>s cannot freely interchange axial and equatorial <acetate> simply by an inexpensive <ring inversion>. <protonation> activates the anomeric acetoxy leaving group. As its C–O bond cleaves to release <acetic acid>, a <lone pair> on the endocyclic <oxygen> donates into the developing anomeric orbital, producing an <oxocarbenium ion>. <water> attacks that <carbon>, and <deprotonation> gives the cyclic <hemiacetal>. Attack from either face accounts for the unspecified anomeric configuration of OH in the <product (chemistry)>.

The axial C–OAc bond in E can adopt <antiperiplanar geometry> relative to an endocyclic <oxygen> <lone pair>. This favourable $n_O\rightarrow\sigma^*_{C-O}$ interaction assists departure in the ionization <transition state>. The corresponding bond in F is poorly aligned in its preferred chair; achieving comparable overlap requires an unfavourable chair change, placing tert-butyl axial, or substantial ring distortion. The distinction is <stereoelectronic control of acetal hydrolysis>, rather than merely counting steric crowding in the <product (chemistry)>s.

An axial <acetal> can itself receive ground-state stabilization from the anomeric effect, so “axial means unstable” is not an adequate rate argument. What matters is the relative <Gibbs energy of activation> for the departing bond and the geometry of lone-pair assistance. Conformationally locked model <acetals> experimentally demonstrate faster <acid (chemistry)>-catalysed cleavage of the axially oriented <isomer>, although their numerical rate ratios should not be transferred to these different <substrate (chemistry)>s.<Stereoelectronic control of the hydrolysis of a conformationally locked acetal by Anthony J. Kirby and Robert J. Martin>[1983, abstract and pp. 1627–1632, acid-catalysed axial/equatorial comparison]{ref}

